    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The leading Welsh kingdom, based on the mountains of Snowdonia and the grain lands of Anglesey, with its chief royal court at Aberffraw.</li><li>Its rulers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a>, repeatedly brought much of Wales under their authority.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> was recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> in 1267, but Edward I conquered Gwynedd in 1282–1283.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A sixth-century gravestone at Penmachno calls the dead man a citizen of ‘Venedotis’, one of the earliest written records of the name of Gwynedd.</p></aside>
    </section>

